Subject: Gauss-Kronrod quadrature use and accuracy
Message-ID: <qt9Z6oSQvc4.fhveRhhsBQAJ@tmb-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/tmb-users/c/qt9Z6oSQvc4/m/fhveRhhsBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello all,

I've been attempting to fit a latent variable model for which the marginal likelihood cannot be approximated using Laplace's method. The code included below I have scraped together from the existing examples, to give an impression of what I'm trying to accomplish, in the hope that clarifies things.
As part of a log-likelihood derived using Variational Approximations, I would like to evaluate an expectation that lacks a closed form expression (as far as I can tell): E(1/exp(eta)), eta = z*theta + z^2*theta2, is the linear predictor and z~ MVRN(a_i, A_i). This is evaluated, i = 1...n times as part of the log-likelihood.

I've been trying to do this using TMB's adaptive Gauss-Kronrod implementation, but seem to be falling short. As I have nothing to compare the results of the numerical integration to for the above example, I've instead compared the results of the numerical integration with E(exp(eta)) for which I do have a closed form expression. Though the integration results are accurate (similar) at the start of the optimization (3 or so decimals at least), for some i the result of the numerical integration is quite poor, i.e. it deviates from the closed-form answer quite rapidly into the optimization, eventually resulting in a negative, negative log-likelihood (i.e. a positive log-likelihood, where the closed-form expression performs just fine) I've gone over my code a dozen times, and can't seem to find mistakes.

Four issues I would greatly appreciate help with/input on:
1) Though the original TMB example using multivariate Romberg integration shows how to pass a vector of a dynamic size to the function, the example with adaptive Gauss-Kronrod quadrature does not. Is it possible to do this, rather than with wrt()?
2) I couldn't get the script to compile when passing TMB-type vectors to the integrand (so this is different from above as that relates to a question for passing a vector to mvIntegrate, rather than having to wrt() for each of the latent variables). So, instead, I used single column matrices. Is this supposed to work with  TMB-type vectors?
3) I don't seem to be able to use REGISTER_ATOMIC() due to not being able to pass vectors to the integrand (I think), which I had hoped to reduce the RAM usage of MakeADFun(), though please correct me if I'm wrong as I have no clue as to the effect of tape size on RAM usage. Are there alternative ways to reduce memory usage (spikes) during AD (note that my code is not parallelized at this time).
4) Is there any other possibility for me to improve the accuracy of the results of the numerical integration? I've attempted to increase the relative and absolute tolerances, but that only resulted in a higher RAM usage.

Bert

//eval below is evaluated for every i = 1...n.
template<class Float>
struct integrand {
 typedef Float Scalar; // Required by integrate
 matrix<Float> theta1;
 matrix<Float> theta2;
 matrix<Float> a;
 matrix<Float> A;

 //z(z1,z2) ~ NMVRN(a,A)
 Float z1; //Latent variable 1
 Float z2; //Latent variable 2

 Float operator() () {

 matrix <Float> z(1,A.cols());
 z(0,0) = z1;
 z(0,1) = z2;

 Float ans = 0;
 ans += (z.array()*theta1.array()).sum() - (z.array()*z.array()*theta2.array()).sum();
 //Subtract negative log-likelihood for the Multivariate Normal as I want the log-likelihood to be added (i.e. int exp(eta(z))h(z)d(z), where h(z) is the Multivariate normal
 ans -= density::MVNORM(A)(z.row(0)-a.row(0));
 ans = exp(ans);

 return ans;
 }

 // Integrate wrt (z1,z2)
 Float integrate() {
 using namespace gauss_kronrod;
 Float ans = mvIntegrate(*this, control(100,1e-5,1e-5)).//Subdivisions, reltol, abstol
 wrt(z1, -INFINITY, INFINITY).
 wrt(z2, -INFINITY, INFINITY) ();
 return ans;
 }
};

template<class Float>
Float eval(matrix<Float> theta1, matrix<Float> theta2, matrix<Float> a, matrix<Float> A){
 integrand<Float> f = {theta1, theta2, a, A};
 return f.integrate();
}

